but first it's not being the eve but  first it's not been the easiest campaign  for the lib dems either the poll  suggests they're being squeezed by the  two main parties as we approach polling  day on thursday  well joining us now is their party  leader jay swinson thank you very much  for being  with us always good to chat with you  sophie how do you think the campaign is  going  well i'm looking forward to liberal  democrats making real progress  of the election on thursday more liberal  democrat mps  and we still have the opportunity to  stop  boris johnson getting a majority to  force through his  hard brexit deal with the real prospect  that we could be out of the eu  with no deal at all in just over a year  so the next few days will be  absolutely crucial as people make up  their minds in marginal seats across the  country  it feels like you're striking a  different tone to the one  that when we last spoke to be completely  frank last month on the show you said  i'm the candidate to be prime minister  at this election absolutely  well i'm technically still a candidate  to be prime minister obviously i'm the  liberal democrat candidate for prime  minister but now you just want to make  progress  but clearly things have changed since  the beginning of the campaign  you know when we started out we had four  political parties that had all been  floating around 20 in the polls and with  a  first-past-the-post voting system that  created a massive  opportunity and so absolutely right to  be very bold in going for that  what we've seen in the intervening weeks  is  a cozy stitch up between nigel farage  and boris johnson  those two parties now sort of  indistinguishable  and obviously that's very worrying to to  many people even those who have  supported the conservatives that now  boris johnson is nigel farage's  candidate so you've had a cozy stitch up  as well with the green party we've  applied  you you've got one you're right well i  absolutely i have worked with other  uh remain supporting parties and i'm  very proud to have done so because the  threat that we face  to our country to opportunities for  future generations from brexit is  very significant and that's why we have  worked on a cross-party basis  with other parties that want to remain  but that stitch up between  farage and johnson clearly changes the  electoral arithmetic and makes it more  difficult  um with that in mind it it feels in some  ways the  best chance the lib dems having any  influence at the election after the  election is if there's a hung parliament  scenario  if there is what would your tactics be  because i know you don't want boris  johnson in number 10 you've also said  you don't want jeremy corbyn at number  10  but what is your actual tactics when  you're faced with the reality  of the situation well we'll find out  obviously on friday morning what the  arithmetic is what has happened in the  election  and jeremy corbyn look i've said for  many reasons jeremy corbyn  is not fit to be prime minister neither  is boris johnson we've seen  in the papers today further evidence of  why that is you know 130  cases of anti-semitism in the labour  party that are outstanding you know  a party that has really failed to get to  grip  with anti-semitism under his watch as  leader that is not somebody who i could  put into  number ten there's clearly a lot of  people within the labour party who i do  work well with and have worked well with  in the last two and a half three and a  half years we have been working on a  cross-party basis to try to secure  a people's vote and that kind of  cooperation  will absolutely continue so you'd  potentially i'm just trying to read  between the lines  here are you asking for like a kind of  caretaker prime minister role if  there is a hunger i'm very open-minded  liberal democrat mps will be  absolutely focused on stopping brexit  and the more liberal democrat mps that  there are the more likely it is that we  will be able to achieve that  and of course we will work and continue  to work  with clyde country with the green party  with the smp with labor and  conservatives  who want to stop brexit put into number  10 then i'm not going to be drawing  names because we don't know what the  scenarios are going to be but i'm  setting out what our approach is going  to be  so we will be absolutely working to stop  brexit  doing so in a in a cooperative way with  others who share our values and share  that goal  and i guess what some people listen to  this might be saying is  if you acknowledge now that it's pretty  unlikely you're actually going to be  prime minister if you won't put boris  johnson into power but you also wouldn't  put jeremy corbyn into power  what's the point of voting the dem well  the point of voting liberal democrat is  to have more libbed mmps  who will work to stop brexit and in very  many  labour who won a second friend i mean  and in very many parts of the country  it's liberal democrats who are taking on  the conservatives  and whether those uh labor-minded voters  decide to back the liberal democrats in  lots of seats will help to determine  whether or not boris johnson has  a majority or whether they elect hard  brexiteers like dominic rob or john  redwood where liberal democrats can  unseat people like that so  you know the the decisions that people  make in the next few days  are really going to be important to  determine whether or not  boris johnson has a majority you said  that you want to make progress in this  election  if you end up with fewer mps than you  had when the election was called  would you then resign no i was  elected as leader of the liberal  democrats four months ago  and that's a big job to be done and you  know four months in you know i've  made a start i've already secured more  mps because of course we had  12 mps at the last general election and  we now became  leader of the party uh but there's a  there's a lot that we need to do there's  a liberal movement to build  we have backwards well look we have i  mean we have a  voting system that can you know we all  know  doesn't favor smaller parties but if we  look at the  range of um metrics the liberal democrat  party is in good health we have more  members than we have ever had in our  history of more than 30 years as a  political party  there is a movement out there of people  who share our  liberal values who want an inclusive  fair and open society  and that is what we need to build and  that is what i am focused on doing  and that's not a job that gets done in  four months bluntly sophie and so i'm  i'm i'm  absolutely here to stay and i'm excited  about the future you talk about the  voting system you're talking about nigel  farage's decision to stand down against  conservative candidates but i just  wonder  if there's a few kind of self-inflicted  mistakes that the dems have made as well  and your policy to revoke article 50  if you actually end up winning an  election so effectively cancelling  brexit i just wonder why did you decide  to adopt that did you look at polling  and  people said it was a good policy what  was the evidence behind that well i mean  it's a democratic decision by conference  it is a good policy because it's honest  about what we would do we are a party  that wants to stop brexit and  we have set out how we would do that  we've been campaigning for a people's  vote  and if we elected a lib dem majority  government obviously we would do that by  revoking article 50.  it's also a policy which which is  popular i mean if you if you look at the  yougov polls you know for people who  want to remain that's the the preferred  policy  in indeed even among labor uh remain  voters it's more popular than the labor  policy  not everyone was convinced they were  they i mean we just have a look at vince  cable for example um  the person of course who you took over  from as a party leader  he said the revoke was a distraction and  not a very helpful one uh  norman lam another of your very  long-standing mps said we are playing  with fire in many ways  and i have to say when i go around the  country it's something that comes up  quite a lot this revoke  policy and if people are perhaps put off  by jeremy corbyn and boris johnson  because they see them as  extreme candidates why did you adopt  what many people would consider to be an  extreme brexit position  well our position is that we want to  remain in the european union  and some people will think that that is  an extreme position but i think it is  about the kind of future that we want to  see for our country doing without a  referendum  is what some would see as extreme well  it's doing it through an election  so you know it is only in the  circumstances where we win a majority  which would be a democratic event  which i think many people would  recognize would be a an electoral  earthquake and therefore would have  democratic legitimacy so i i recognize  that there's people who criticize this  policy but you know generally there are  people  who want to leave the european union now  you as well have been kind of front and  center  of your campaign it's been you on the  front of the buses it's been your  picture on the leaflets  was it a mistake to run such a  presidential style of campaign  do you know what sophie i've fought lots  of different general elections and  generally it is the leader of the party  whose face is on the bus and whose  picture is on the leaflets i mean that  is what being a leader of a political  party  uh is is like and it's it's exactly what  i experienced when charles kennedy  was leader when nick clegg was leader  and so on so you know it isn't  it doesn't mean everybody i mean the  labour party for example you wouldn't  necessarily say that jeremy corbyn is on  front of every bus and every leaflet  well and you know he he he is not and  there's a story as to why that is  right and that tells you something about  even how many people in the labour party  recognize that  you know jeremy corbyn is somebody who  whether it's because he's  neutral on brexit refusing to take a  leadership position whether it's his  failure to tackle anti-semitism those  people have very real concerns about  jeremy corbyn and we can look at some of  the polling  around your approval ratings as well and  this is  um polling that we've seen uh done by  ugarven sky news  and you can see there the you know it's  almost quite difficult to kind of bring  up but you know the unfavorable ratings  they're  ticking up and up the favorable rating  staying low or dropping slightly  it almost feels like the more people see  you they the less they like you i mean  is that that  we're in an election and i'm taking a  very clear position  on brexit i want to remain in the  european union  and you know i do recognize that some  people aren't going to like that they  might not  like what i say on brexit some people  don't like what i say that i want  scotland to stay  in the united kingdom uh some people you  know don't like the way i talk or you  know what my shoes look like or whatever  else  but you know what i'm going to still  stand up for what i believe in  because i want to change things i  believe that our future can be better  our country can be better  and that is what i'm working for and  yeah some people will like it  many people do like it millions of  people want to see that future  and some people don't and that's fine  that's politics do you think some of it  is a bit sexist because you hear people  talking about  you sound like a head girl you sound a  bit bossy you sound a bit angry  sexist look i mean sophie you and i both  um  you know exist within public life where  we recognize that there are double  standards that  apply and you know i i knew that when i  took on the job  and i want things to change because if i  think about you know my  nieces growing up i want the world that  they inherit to be one that is less  sexist and one of the ways that we can  do that  is by having women in leadership roles  um blazing a trail  and i'm delighted and proud to be doing  that okay joe swinson thank you very  much for coming on the show this morning  and setting up your pitch for the  election in just a few days time thank  you  thank you

